Ok, now run the ECU (EISA Configuration Utility) that is appropriate for the
OS (Operating Systems) that you will be running.

This may in fact fix your CD-ROM issue, the EISA setup could be confused, or
check the cabling.

I cannot stress enough, you must run the ECU on Alpha systems, whether they
have EISA or not.

The Alpha hardware was designed such that most of the embedded hardware is
configured by the ECU. This goes for Serial Ports, Floppy, IDE, Parrallel
ports, Remote Management ports, Built-in Display, Ethernet interface and
other stuff.

So some people think they get away with not running the ECU. Hah! The guys
says to me, I have a bunch of machines and I cannot see any of the floppy
drives on any systems when Tru64 UNIX is running, I want them all replaced.
