In article <2hi82pFdaf8rU1@uni-berlin.de>, "Robert A. Healey" <healer@rpi.edu> writes:
:I've inherited the task of keeping an older VaxStation running.  I've 
:been attempting to find a manual for it somewhere, but everywhere I 
:look, this model appears to not exist.  The front panel declares it to 
:be a Vaxstation 4.  

  VAXstation 4 was typically an upgraded system; an existing Q-bus system
  with a KA650 or KA655 series processor module was upgraded by replacing
  the processor with a KA660 series processor; with the same processor 
  used in the VAX 4000 model 200 series.  

  The VAX 4 upgrade part number was 2T-KA660-*, with the suffix depending
  on the particular licensing (timeshare, single-user or server) that was
  the target for the upgraded system.  Your upgrade likely includes the
  KA660-BA series processor module; the VAXstation or single-user module.
  For multi-user or timeshare, the processor module is the KA660-AA series.

  You can largely treat the system as a VAX 4000 model 200 series, save
  for the physical enclosure and the front labels.  You have the BA23 or
  BA123 series Q-bus enclosure, where the enclosures most commonly used
  for the KA660 were from the BA4xx series. 

  When acquiring Q-bus I/O cards for this configuration, you'll typically
  want to acquire cards for the BA23 or BA123 series boxes and not cards
  for the BA2xx or BA4xx series enclosures (the I/O bulkhead differs),
  and you'll need to follow the Q-bus Q22/CD and Q22/Q22 slot rules for
  the the BA23 or the BA123 series boxes; not the rules for the Q-bus
  within the BA2xx and BA4xx series enclosures.

  For some information related to Q-bus configuration rules for various
  boxes, search for the keyword "serpentine" in the Ask The Wizard area.


  The following assumes OpenVMS, though other operating systems can also
  be commonly discussed within this comp.sys.dec newsgroup...

  The OpenVMS minimum version for the KA660 series is V5.4-2, and this
  system -- assuming sufficient physical memory and disk storage -- is
  considered supported on the current OpenVMS VAX V7.3 release.

