In article <2hmaaoFefpcgU1@uni-berlin.de>, "Robert A. Healey" <healer@rpi.edu> writes:
:...The systems collection ... is mainly machines saved from the junk heap.
:Besides the Vaxstation, there is a VAX 8530 (no cdrom on it)...  

  There were a few Q-bus CD-ROM controllers around for Q-bus systems,
  via KZQSA, RQD50/RRD50 or HSZ05.  Most VAXstation systems can support
  the hardware needed to connect to and boot from CD-ROM, in other words.
  SCSI is slightly more difficult, particularly disk.  HSZ05 is one of
  the few approaches there.  InfoServer bootstraps are also possible.

  The VAX 8530, on the other hand, requires a magtape or InfoServer
  bootstrap, as there is no easy way to connect such a system with a 
  CD-ROM for the initial system load (ISL).  There is also no easy way
  to connect it to SCSI disk storage, short of a CI star and an HSJ 
  series controller -- and I haven't tried that -- which is a whole
  lot of hardware.

:I have a friend with access to an Alpha if that helps.  

  Only if it has compatible media with the other systems, such as
  a TSZ07 9 track or DLT or such.

:I was thinking of calling HP when I get back to school and seeing if they
:have any distribution sets still on magnetic tape (any version).

  We have largely (or completely?) retired the 9 track and DLT (TK50)
  distributions.  I don't know if there are any old magtape or old DLT
  distributions stacked up somewhere in a shipping department, but I'd
  not count on it.  AFAIK, we retired all magnetic media distributions
  a while back.

  Since you're working with this ancient hardware, you'll want to determine
  how you can replicate the distribution kits around.  Compatible media, or
  compatible devices, or hardware device swaps.

  The core of that replication work is locating common media and/or locating
  and configuring common storage devices.

  You might find a used distro on the surplus market, or via a friend.

  Or you might find a used InfoServer somewhere, and load OpenVMS VAX 
  from that.  (There is work underway to add InfoServer support into 
  base OpenVMS, but the results are unlikely to be available in your 
  desired timeframe.)

  The ISL difficulties and the lack of media availability are the 
  central reason why many of the older VAX systems are moving toward
  (or have reached) an unsupported-hardware status.

  You might try comp.os.vms, as folks over there might have kits
  you could acquire -- all this assumes the software licenses and
  related agreements permit copying of media, etc., of course.


