The VT510 is an RS-232 (actually RS-423) async serial terminal, not a
network device.  If it is currently connected to a DECServer (which model?
200,300,700,90x?) then the VT terminals use serial lines to the DECServer,
which in turn connects to your network via a separate 10baseT or something
like that.

If the VTs are connected to digiboards (sounds like an old SCO system) then
there is no DECserver; Digiboards typically were multi-port RS-232
interface, no ethernet used.  However you can use point to point CAT 5 to
carry RS-232.  Is that what you are trying to accomplish?  You'll need an
RJ45 and MMJ crimping tool to make the cables.

You'll need to be a bit more specific as to how the DECserver is involved as
your description is conflicting at the moment.
  Jack Peacock




