In article <40A002D0.15.dec@alexbbs.dyndns.org>, "Alex" <alex@alexbbs.dyndns.org.remove-104i-this> writes:

:I came across a reference to hooking the 5.25in floppy drive from a pc up 
:to a vax somewhere in the netbsd docs. unfortunately it didn't supply much 
:info on the subject.
:Is this in fact possible? if so what hardware is needed on the vax side? 
:can the drive be hooked directly into the rqdx* controler? the end of the 
:cable you hook to the disk looks the same as the connecter on the 5.25in 
:floppy.

  I'll assume this is with the MicroVAX II system mentioned in your
  other posting(s).

  Try it, assuming you don't mind the chance of smoking the RQDXx.  (If
  the floppy smokes, who cares?  They're cheap.)  Remember that if the
  drive light comes on continuously, the cabling may be inverted in the
  connector -- the cable keying was occasionally a little weird on these
  ancient systems.

  The usual floppy drive for these systems was the RX50 dual-disk floppy
  drive.  This connected to the bulkhead behind the drive, and from there
  along to the RQDXx controller.

  There was also a SCSI variant of a floppy around for a while, but that
  drive was not commonly seen on the MicroVAX II series -- one of the
  more difficult parts of working with the MicroVAX II at present is the
  lack of a good, direct, native SCSI interface, and the difficulty of
  swapping devices between this and other systems.  Well, that and the
  "fun" of getting the Q-bus CSRs and interrupt vectors set correctly.

  There is also an RRD50 and an RQD50 CD-ROM option on some of these
  boxes, or -- specifically for older CD-ROM devices and older tapes
  -- the KZQSA Q-bus to SCSI controller.  KZQSA is not reliable with
  magnetic disks nor faster SCSI devices, and not recommended for same.
  The other approach is an HSD-series controller and a KFQSA adapter,
  but the KFQSA identifies itself to the console and to OpenVMS as zero
  or more UDA-style disk devices (depending on the number of disk widgets
  on the DSSI bus, or on the SCSI behind the HSD controller) and not as a
  more current DK-style SCSI interface.


